<section
  class='fullscreen-section'
  id='about'
  data-title='About — Katja Turnsek | UX/UI Designer & Front-End Developer'
  data-description='UX/UI designer and front-end developer with a visual-design background, creating responsive, accessible digital experiences from concept to code.'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <h2>Designing with Intention, Building with Care</h2>

      <p class='align-right-60'>
        I’m a
        <strong>UX/UI designer and front-end developer</strong>
        with a professional background in visual design, branding, illustration, and digital
        interfaces. I bring design and code together to create responsive experiences with
        <strong>clear structure</strong>,
        <strong>accessible interactions</strong>, and a
        <strong>strong visual identity</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h3>My Story</h3>

      <p>
        My path began in visual design and art, with many years of professional work spanning
        branding, illustration, web design, touchscreen interfaces, and digital layouts. That
        experience taught me to notice the details that shape an experience: hierarchy, spacing,
        typography, colour, rhythm, and how people move through an interface.
      </p>

      <p>
        Wanting to take ideas beyond visual concepts and turn them into working digital products, I
        studied front-end development at Noroff. Today, I use
        <strong>semantic HTML</strong>,
        <strong>modern CSS</strong>,
        <strong>JavaScript</strong>,
        <strong>TypeScript</strong>,
        <strong>React</strong>, and
        <strong>REST APIs</strong>
        to build responsive and accessible interfaces. My recent projects include booking, auction,
        e-commerce, and portfolio experiences with reusable components, authentication, and
        API-driven content.
      </p>

      <p>
        What I enjoy most is working where design and development meet: shaping the idea, refining
        the user experience, and then building it into something real. I like interfaces that feel
        calm, clear, and easy to use while still having a distinct visual identity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Outside digital work, I paint
        <strong>fine-art pet portraits</strong>
        in oils. Whether I work with pixels, code, or paint, I care about the same things:
        <strong>clarity</strong>,
        <strong>craft</strong>, and
        <strong>human connection</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
